course: dictionary
lesson: 6111
title: Mick: a name, a phrase, and a word to avoid
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mick is a short form of the name Michael, so you will find it in stories as a character's name. «Uncle Mick bets £20 every Saturday.» Here Mick is a person, just like Ben or Sam. A capital M at the start is a clue that it is a name.
explain: In British, Irish and Australian speech, to «take the mick» (or «take the mickey») means to tease someone or make fun of them. «Are you taking the mick?» asks whether someone is teasing you. It can be friendly or unkind, so look at the tone and the situation.
explain: Mick is also an offensive word for an Irish person. Never use it that way. If you hear it used to insult someone, do not join in, and tell a grown-up you trust. The mean tone and the situation are your clues, so you know it is wrong.
fact: Mick is a short form of the name Michael.
fact: To «take the mick» or «take the mickey» means to tease or make fun of someone.
fact: «Are you taking the mick?» is a question asking whether someone is teasing you.
fact: Over 52 weeks, £20 a week makes £1,040, so someone who wins back £200 has still lost £840.
fact: Mick is an offensive word for an Irish person. Never use it that way.
exercise: «Uncle Mick bets £20 every Saturday.» In this sentence, what is Mick? | A long walk | *A person's name | A bag of money | A way of teasing someone
exercise: Sam says, «Are you taking the mick?» What is Sam asking? | Are you going to the shops? | Are you lost? | *Are you teasing me? | Are you cold?
exercise: Which one is a short form of the name Michael? | Max | Mia | Maya | *Mick
test: Which phrase means teasing someone? | *Take the mick | Take a nap | Take a break | Take a bus
test: «Uncle Mick bets £20 every Saturday.» Over 52 weeks, how much does he bet in total? | *£1,040 | £840 | £200 | £520
exam: The word mick is offensive when used about Irish people. What should you do? | *Never use it, and tell a trusted adult if you hear it used like that | Say it loudly so everyone hears | Use it when you want to be funny
